result.reasoning:
I could not refute this; it is a genuine defect, and the repository contains a pristine reference implementation that proves it.

Verified chain:

1. scheduler() does NOT switch stacks. /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/kernel/sched/sched.c:53-103 only updates current_running[cpu_id], current_cpu_running, screen cursors, and satp. There is no switch_to anywhere in the tree (grep over *.c/*.h/*.S finds none). The actual context restore is in /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/arch/riscv/kernel/entry.S:155-158 (ret_from_exception: `ld tp, current_cpu_running` + RESTORE_CONTEXT), reached only because exception_handler_entry does `la ra, ret_from_exception` (entry.S:205) before jumping to interrupt_helper. So every statement after a scheduler() call executes immediately on the still-current kernel stack. The reviewer's premise is exactly correct.

2. The author documented this convention himself. /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/kernel/sched/sched.c:378-386 comment on do_waitpid: "A blocked process can only be resumed in user mode, so waitpid cannot simply continue where it left off... The return value has to be `pid` in that case because handle_syscall() writes it back into a0, which is the very argument the retried `ecall` will pick up." do_waitpid rewinds `regs->sepc = regs->sepc - 4` (sched.c:416). binsem_op (kernel/locking/binsem.c:47-54) has the UNLOCKER fill in the heir's binsem_id rather than letting the woken task do it. Both confirm the reviewer's characterization of the codebase convention.

3. The syscall return value is written into the blocked task's saved a0. /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/kernel/syscall/syscall.c:9 does `regs->regs[10] = syscall[regs->regs[17]](...)`, where `regs` is the caller's kernel-stack frame captured at entry (`mv a0, sp`, entry.S:208). So do_net_recv's `return rx_tail` (net.c:93) lands in the now-blocked task's a0 and is what it sees on resume.

4. Nothing has filled recv_stage at the time of the copy loop. The only writer is XEmacPsRecvHandler (/workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/drivers/emacps/xemacps_main.c:915-947, writing via _addr/_frLength), reachable only from handle_xemacps_irq <- handle_irq (kernel/irq/irq.c:71). Interrupts are masked by disable_preempt() at net.c:46 (`csrw CSR_SIE, zero`, entry.S:123-129) until enable_preempt() at net.c:81 -- three instructions before the copy loop at net.c:86-91. So recv_stage/recv_stage_len are still zero: rx_tail == 0, every *(frLength+i) == 0, user buffer untouched. Exactly the symptom claimed.

DECISIVE EVIDENCE: /workspace/Project6-File_System/drivers/net.c is pristine in this repo (`git status --porcelain Project6-File_System/drivers/` returns empty -- the agent did not modify it; only fs.h, syscall_number.h, stdio.h, fs/disk.c, fs/fs.c, mm/mm.c, sched/sched.c are dirty in Project6). That baseline implements the same function correctly:
    _addr = get_kva_of(addr, (uintptr_t)current_running[cpu_id]->pgdir);
    _num_packet = num_packet;
    _frLength = (size_t*)get_kva_of((uintptr_t)frLength, (uintptr_t)current_running[cpu_id]->pgdir);
    do_block(&current_running[cpu_id]->list, &net_recv_queue);
    disable_sum();
    scheduler();
    return 0;
It pre-translates the USER buffer to a kernel VA so the interrupt handler writes directly into the caller's memory through the kernel direct map, and performs NO copy after scheduler(). This refutes the rationale in the Project5 comment (net.c:26-32) that the handler "cannot touch user memory at all" -- get_kva_of is available in Project5 as well, at /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/arch/riscv/include/pgtable.h:133. The staging-buffer scheme was unnecessary and its copy-out is placed where the data provably has not yet arrived.

Corroborating asymmetry: do_net_send's interrupt path (net.c:119-124) is fine because it returns void and needs no post-scheduler data; poll-mode recv (net.c:56-71) is fine because EmacPsWaitRecv busy-waits before copying. Only interrupt-mode recv reads state produced after the function has already returned.

Reachability: interrupt mode is a core graded requirement of this lab. SYSCALL_NET_RECV is wired at /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/init/main.c:167; /workspace/Project5-Device_Driver/test/recv.c:19-24 selects mode 1 from argv[2] and lines 29-40 print the return value and hexdump using recv_length[i] -- which with this code prints 0 and an empty dump.

Every candidate refutation angle fails: the path is reachable, the test exercises it, the reviewer did not misread any macro or pointer type, the behavior is not handled elsewhere, and the correct fix pattern already exists in the sibling project directory using a helper that is present in Project5. Therefore refuted = false.
